  "script": "Why is Harry Kane's Ballon d'Or so SPECIAL? When Kane was about eight or nine, Arsenal's academy let him go because they thought he was too chubby and not athletic enough, and there's even a photo of him as a kid in the Arsenal kit. After that, he went back to a local club in East London before Tottenham signed him at 11, and even they weren't sold on him. So he got loaned out four times, and most people saw him as a squad player. His breakout didn't come until he was 21, when he scored 31 goals in one season and became one of the best strikers on the planet. When his time at Tottenham finally came to an end, Kane left them as their all-time top scorer with 267 goals, but zero trophies. To make things worse, he lost the Euro 2020 final and the Euro 2024 final, so the trophyless label followed him everywhere and he was often mocked by fans online over it. Then things finally changed when he joined Bayern Munich and won back-to-back Bundesliga titles and a domestic double, while scoring 73 goals for club and country in one single season. At this year's Ballon d'Or ceremony, which was the first one ever held in the UK, moved to London specifically to honor Stanley Matthews, the Englishman who won the very first Ballon d'Or in 1956. 70 years later, Harry Kane just became the first English player to win the Ballon d'Or in 25 years since Michael Owen in 2001, and the first Bayern player to win it in 45 years.",
